INT. CARGO WAREHOUSE - LOADING BAY 6 - NIGHT

Concrete. Pallets. Sodium lights buzzing. A forklift idles.

AGENT FOLASADE ADEBAYO, 30s, FBI jacket under a rain slicker, moves between shipping crates with her weapon low. Behind her, REUBEN MARSH, 50s, local PD, wheezes, flashlight in hand.

REUBEN
Fola, wait.

FOLA
Keep your voice down.

REUBEN
Nobody's here.

FOLA
Somebody dropped this.

She holds up a ring of keys. Reuben shines the light on them.

A STRANGER, 40s, in a dark wool coat, stands motionless at the end of the aisle. Both swing their guns toward him.

STRANGER
Easy. Easy.

FOLA
Hands where I can see them.

STRANGER
They're empty.

REUBEN
Name.

STRANGER
You first.

Fola takes a step forward. The Stranger smiles.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
Agent Adebayo. You look tired.

FOLA
How do you know my name?

STRANGER
I've read your reports.

REUBEN
What reports?

FOLA
Reuben, don't.

STRANGER
Detective Marsh. You should ask her about Lagos.

Reuben's light wavers on Fola's face.

REUBEN
What's in Lagos?

FOLA
Nothing. He's playing us.

STRANGER
Or I'm the only honest one here.

IMELDA DRAY, 50s, steps out of the shadow behind Reuben, a silenced pistol pressed to the back of his neck. She is in a janitor's smock, calm as a clerk.

IMELDA
Drop it, Detective.

Reuben drops the flashlight. It rolls.

FOLA
Imelda. Walk away.

IMELDA
I can't. Not until I get the ledger.

FOLA
I don't have it.

IMELDA
No. He does.

All three look at the Stranger. He taps his coat.

STRANGER
My name is Nikolai Orlov. I'd like to hand it over, if you'll all stop pointing things at me.

NIKOLAI
(CONT'D)
The ledger names every one of you.

REUBEN
Fola, what does he mean every one of us?

FOLA
It means somebody in this room is on the take.

IMELDA
Or two.

NIKOLAI
Or three.

Silence. Rain on the roof.

REUBEN
I didn't do anything.

NIKOLAI
Not yet.

Imelda cocks the pistol.
